From 55264df21e03910eab1a3da6bac20af6c45bc970 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 25 Nov 2026 04:51:57 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [Hades][GLES] The 3D LUT read off the render thread and put
 on the GPU a part a frame

A LUT file was read, converted and kept on the render thread, in the
frame that first needed it, and Kodi's renderer then copied the whole
table into a texture layout in that frame too: a madVR LUT (256 points
a side, a file of up to 256 MB and 200 MB of floats) stalled that frame
for as long as it took, with the file, the table and the copy in memory
at once (about 650 MB).

The window system now reads and converts the files on a thread of a
loader of its own (CLutLoader, DisplayLutLoader.h): the bytes of a file
are let go as soon as its table is made, one file is read at a time,
and asking for another file of a kind lets go of the table of before
first, so that at most a table of each kind and one file being read are
in memory. The render thread never waits for it: while a file is read,
the picture goes without the LUT and the player information says
"loading" (#62821, Estuary #54821); a read whose file is no longer
asked for is never given. The bridges let go of a table before the
loader lets it go.

Kodi's renderer makes no copy of the table any more: it lays the
texture out (dvbridge_lut_atlas_layout()) and puts the slices of blue
in it straight from the table, 16 MB at most a frame
(dvbridge_lut_atlas_slice(), CLut3dGLES::Begin() and Put()); until all
of it is there the shaders do not take it, and the window system is
told that it is loading, not that it failed. The texels are those of
dvbridge_lut_atlas_make(), so the pictures are the same, to the bit.

libplacebo still puts the bridge's own copy of a table on the GPU in
the first frame that applies it (pl_shader_custom_lut()): that is
libplacebo's, and is not changed here.
